import{_ as r,E as i,h as n,c as o,b as a,f as s,H as c,a4 as d}from"./chunks/framework.B_fYP6Of.js";const w=JSON.parse('{"title":"Project brief","description":"Follow a portable research library from an imported record to a cited answer. Explore the proposed system, its first demonstration and the current implementation boundary.","frontmatter":{"title":"Project brief","description":"Follow a portable research library from an imported record to a cited answer. Explore the proposed system, its first demonstration and the current implementation boundary.","teaser":"Two accounts name the same incident and disagree about the date. The first job of a useful research library is to let a reader see both accounts."},"headers":[],"relativePath":"project/index.md","filePath":"project/index.md"}'),l={name:"project/index.md"};function p(h,e,u,m,b,f){const t=i("LabArticleHeader");return n(),o("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=a("h1",{id:"project-brief",tabindex:"-1"},[s("Project brief "),a("a",{class:"header-anchor",href:"#project-brief","aria-label":'Permalink to "Project brief"'},"​")],-1)),c(t),e[1]||(e[1]=d('<p>Imagine importing two reports about a single event. One gives a date the other disputes. The intended library can identify the shared incident, retain the two claims and let a learner open the lines of evidence behind each. That small example explains why the project starts with identity, source records and review.</p><p><strong>Ordo Knowledge Lab · IS373 · documentation and contract-review phase.</strong></p><p>Build a portable research library that gains processing capabilities without losing control of its evidence. UFO reports are the first teaching domain, not a permanent restriction on the engine. Build from carefully selected Ordo behaviors rather than importing the entire business platform or starting without its useful code.</p><h2 id="user-experience" tabindex="-1">User experience <a class="header-anchor" href="#user-experience" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;User experience&quot;">​</a></h2><p>An instructor imports sources, reviews ambiguous identities and claims, and publishes a revision. A learner asks a question in a streaming interface and opens the exact evidence behind the answer. The same published library opens on another machine even when a processing plugin is unavailable.</p><p>The central principle is <strong>canonical identity, not canonical truth</strong>. One incident may have conflicting accounts. Resolving its identity does not authorize erasing disagreement.</p><h2 id="current-direction" tabindex="-1">Current direction <a class="header-anchor" href="#current-direction" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Current direction&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Rust owns state and coordination. Workers use language-neutral contracts and may be Rust, Swift or another appropriate implementation. CPU/NVIDIA/Apple capabilities are optional. The data is portable; jobs are durable and observable. Identity resolution, provenance, review and recovery take precedence over infrastructure complexity.</p><p>The hosted API and workers follow <a href="./../engineering/twelve-factor">twelve-factor requirements</a>, with a private Rust store as their durable backing service. Local authoring may embed that store as an explicitly stateful appliance. We do not call a database stateless merely because it runs in a container.</p><h2 id="what-to-review-first" tabindex="-1">What to review first <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-to-review-first" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What to review first&quot;">​</a></h2><p><a href="./../reuse/">Reuse map</a> → <a href="./../reuse/source-audit">source audit</a> → <a href="./system">system</a> → <a href="./knowledge">knowledge</a> → <a href="./../engineering/twelve-factor">factor matrix</a> → <a href="./roadmap">roadmap</a>.</p><p>The <a href="./../engineering/examples">executable examples</a> make specific failure contracts testable. The <a href="./acceptance">requirements</a> describe broader application acceptance that those small labs do not satisfy on their own.</p><h2 id="first-complete-demonstration" tabindex="-1">First complete demonstration <a class="header-anchor" href="#first-complete-demonstration" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;First complete demonstration&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A useful demonstration has a second reader. Give that person the published library on another machine and ask them to find the passage behind one answer. If an optional processor is missing, the existing evidence should still open and the unavailable operation should be identifiable.</p><p>Import synthetic reports containing aliases, two people sharing a name, copied testimony and a contradiction. Repeat the import without duplicate acceptance. Resolve ambiguity, publish a reviewed revision, stream a cited answer, and open the library on Linux without the Mac worker. Inspect what a stopped worker or disconnected browser did and did not preserve.</p><h2 id="boundaries" tabindex="-1">Boundaries <a class="header-anchor" href="#boundaries" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Boundaries&quot;">​</a></h2><p>This repository currently contains documentation, checks and synthetic examples—not the running application. No multi-writer library sync, unrestricted public ingestion, automatic canonical merges, plugin marketplace or production-scale claim is required for the first release. Native video comes later as a bounded capability.</p><p>Portfolio DNS, documentation Pages and the future application host are distinct operations. See <a href="./hosting">hosting</a>, <a href="./decisions">decisions</a>, <a href="./sources">source rules</a> and <a href="./../status">status</a>. This instructor example does not alter course grading or require students to adopt every proposed technology.</p>',18))])}const v=r(l,[["render",p]]);export{w as __pageData,v as default};
